# Dev Confession Blog - Basic Version
# Simple structure with minimal styling

name: "Dev Confession Blog"
resourceType: "website"

screens::
  # Home Screen - Blog Feed
  - id: "home"
    name: "Home"
    isEntryPoint: true
    children::
      - type: "heading"
        content: "💻 DEV CONFESSIONS"
        css: "font-size: 48px; text-align: center; color: #89b4fa; margin-bottom: 20px;"

      - type: "text"
        content: "Anonymous developer confessions and memes"
        css: "text-align: center; color: #8b949e; margin-bottom: 40px;"

      # Blog Post Cards Container
      - type: "section-container"
        name: "blog-cards"
        css: "display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 20px; padding: 20px; max-width: 800px; margin: 0 auto;"
        children::
          # Card 1
          - type: "section-container"
            css: "background: #0d0e13; border: 1px solid #30363d; border-radius: 12px; padding: 20px;"
            children::
              - type: "heading"
                content: "I Use Light Mode"
                css: "font-size: 24px; color: #cba6f7; margin-bottom: 10px;"

              - type: "text"
                content: "And I'm not sorry... (Click to read more)"
                css: "color: #8b949e; margin-bottom: 15px;"

              - type: "nav-button"
                content: "Read More →"
                action: "navigate"
                targetScreen: "post-1"
                css: "background: #89b4fa; color: #010409; padding: 10px 20px; border-radius: 6px; border: none; cursor: pointer;"

          # Card 2
          - type: "section-container"
            css: "background: #0d0e13; border: 1px solid #30363d; border-radius: 12px; padding: 20px;"
            children::
              - type: "heading"
                content: "I Don't Know What DNS Is"
                css: "font-size: 24px; color: #cba6f7; margin-bottom: 10px;"

              - type: "text"
                content: "At this point I'm too afraid to ask..."
                css: "color: #8b949e; margin-bottom: 15px;"

              - type: "nav-button"
                content: "Read More →"
                action: "navigate"
                targetScreen: "post-2"
                css: "background: #89b4fa; color: #010409; padding: 10px 20px; border-radius: 6px; border: none; cursor: pointer;"

          # Card 3
          - type: "section-container"
            css: "background: #0d0e13; border: 1px solid #30363d; border-radius: 12px; padding: 20px;"
            children::
              - type: "heading"
                content: "My Code Works and I Don't Know Why"
                css: "font-size: 24px; color: #cba6f7; margin-bottom: 10px;"

              - type: "text"
                content: "A tutorial on mysterious fixes"
                css: "color: #8b949e; margin-bottom: 15px;"

              - type: "nav-button"
                content: "Read More →"
                action: "navigate"
                targetScreen: "post-3"
                css: "background: #89b4fa; color: #010409; padding: 10px 20px; border-radius: 6px; border: none; cursor: pointer;"

  # Blog Post 1 - Light Mode
  - id: "post-1"
    name: "Post 1 - Light Mode"
    children::
      - type: "section-container"
        css: "max-width: 800px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 40px 20px;"
        children::
          - type: "heading"
            content: "I Use Light Mode (And I'm Not Sorry)"
            css: "font-size: 36px; color: #cba6f7; margin-bottom: 20px;"

          - type: "thread"
            name: "Light Mode Post"
            description: "Satirical defense of light mode"
            mode: "markdown"
            content: """
# I Use Light Mode (And I'm Not Sorry)

Look, I know what you're thinking. You're judging me right now. I can feel the collective gasp of developers everywhere reading this.

## Why Light Mode?

1. **I can read it** - Shocking concept, I know
2. **My eyes don't hurt** - Revolutionary idea
3. **I work during the day** - You know, when there's actual light

## The Dark Side of Dark Mode

Everyone acts like dark mode is the only way to code. "It's easier on the eyes!" they say, squinting at their screens in a pitch-black room at 3 AM.

Meanwhile, I'm here, living my best life with readable text and proper contrast.

## Join the Light Side

We have cookies. And properly lit rooms.
            """

          - type: "nav-button"
            content: "← Back to Confessions"
            action: "navigate"
            targetScreen: "home"
            css: "background: #21262d; color: #c9d1d9; padding: 10px 20px; border-radius: 6px; border: none; cursor: pointer; margin-top: 30px;"

  # Blog Post 2 - DNS
  - id: "post-2"
    name: "Post 2 - DNS"
    children::
      - type: "section-container"
        css: "max-width: 800px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 40px 20px;"
        children::
          - type: "heading"
            content: "I Don't Know What DNS Is"
            css: "font-size: 36px; color: #cba6f7; margin-bottom: 20px;"

          - type: "thread"
            name: "DNS Confession"
            description: "Humorous exploration of tech acronyms"
            mode: "markdown"
            content: """
# I Don't Know What DNS Is (And At This Point I'm Too Afraid to Ask)

It's been 5 years since I started programming. I've built websites, deployed applications, even fixed production bugs.

But if you ask me what DNS actually stands for? I panic.

## Things I Tell Myself

- "It's those server things"
- "Something about domains"
- "The internet phone book?" (Is it though?)

## Other Acronyms I Pretend to Understand

- API (I know this one... I think)
- REST (Definitely about sleeping)
- CORS (The beer, right?)
- JWT (Just... Web... Tokens?)

## The Truth

I Google it every single time. And immediately forget. It's a vicious cycle.

*Note: If you're reading this and you work with me, no you didn't.*
            """

          - type: "nav-button"
            content: "← Back to Confessions"
            action: "navigate"
            targetScreen: "home"
            css: "background: #21262d; color: #c9d1d9; padding: 10px 20px; border-radius: 6px; border: none; cursor: pointer; margin-top: 30px;"

  # Blog Post 3 - Code Works
  - id: "post-3"
    name: "Post 3 - Code Works"
    children::
      - type: "section-container"
        css: "max-width: 800px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 40px 20px;"
        children::
          - type: "heading"
            content: "My Code Works and I Don't Know Why"
            css: "font-size: 36px; color: #cba6f7; margin-bottom: 20px;"

          - type: "thread"
            name: "Mysterious Fixes"
            description: "Tutorial on mysterious bug fixes"
            mode: "markdown"
            content: """
# My Code Works and I Don't Know Why

## The Journey

1. **Hour 1:** Code doesn't work. Obviously.
2. **Hour 2:** Stack Overflow says to try random things
3. **Hour 3:** Nothing works. Considering career change.
4. **Hour 4:** Changed one line. IT WORKS???

## What Did I Change?

Honestly? I have no idea. I added a console.log, removed it, changed a variable name, changed it back, and suddenly everything works.

## The Fear

Now I'm terrified to touch anything. The code is working. I don't know why. If I look at it too hard, it might break again.

## Best Practices

- Don't question it
- Don't touch it
- Commit immediately
- Never look back

*If it ain't broke, don't try to understand it.*
            """

          - type: "nav-button"
            content: "← Back to Confessions"
            action: "navigate"
            targetScreen: "home"
            css: "background: #21262d; color: #c9d1d9; padding: 10px 20px; border-radius: 6px; border: none; cursor: pointer; margin-top: 30px;"
